A view `for … else` over a *constant* iterable, which passViewForElse must not
be the thing that decides at run time. On every target the loop reaches
passViewForElse and is rewritten there like any other, into a conditional whose
test happens to be constant, and the fold after lowering collapses it.

What differs is who unrolls what is left. html's view is markup, so
optimize.Documents unrolls the non-empty loop into its rows and the archive
below holds the empty-state text as markup, with no loop left. A language
target does not unroll -- its own compiler can see the bounds -- which is why
on bubbletea the empty case reads as a plain line and the non-empty loop keeps
its `for` and loses only its unreachable else.

Both readings are the same claim: nothing in this file's output tests
emptiness at run time, because nothing in this file's iterables is unknown
until then. A view for-else that fires passViewForElse for real is
for_else_view.txtar, where the iterable is state.

deny out/none/html/index.html `for \(` -- the static path unrolls both loops away; a `for` reaching the markup would mean the else was decided at run time on a target that has none
deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `len\(\[\]string` -- the emptiness test over a constant iterable does not survive the fold that runs after lowering

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}

const empty list[string] = []

const two list[string] = ["a", "b"]

component main node {
    vbox {
        for var x = empty {
            text(value=x)
        } else {
            text(value="EMPTY CONST")
        }
        for var y = two {
            text(value=y)
        } else {
            text(value="NEVER")
        }
    }
}

window {
    main
}
